#
# xiao-cs-gateway configuration.
#

menu "Channel Sounding gateway"

config CS_GATEWAY_ID
	string "Gateway identifier"
	default "CSGW01"
	help
	  Reported in every serial line, mirroring GATEWAY_ID in the T-Echo
	  gateway's config.h. Note this is a label only — the bridge authenticates
	  to Supabase with the gateway key in its own .env, not with this value.

config CS_GATEWAY_FW_VERSION
	string "Gateway firmware version"
	default "cs-0.1.0"
	help
	  Reported upstream so a distance in the database can be traced back to
	  the firmware that produced it.

config CS_GATEWAY_RANGING_DWELL_MS
	int "Milliseconds to range each tag before moving to the next"
	default 4000
	help
	  With several tags in range this is what bounds how long any one of them
	  can hold the radio. Lower it for fresher distances across many tags,
	  raise it for a longer median window and steadier numbers on few tags.

config CS_GATEWAY_REPORT_MIN_MS
	int "Minimum milliseconds between reported observations for one tag"
	default 1000
	help
	  Channel Sounding produces fresh estimates at roughly 10 Hz. Reporting
	  every one of them would write around 860k rows per tag per day for no
	  extra information, since they all come from the same median window.
	  This paces the output the way the T-Beam tag paces itself with
	  SEND_INTERVAL_MS. Lower it if you need faster-moving distances.

config CS_GATEWAY_SCAN_WINDOW_MS
	int "Milliseconds to scan for tags between ranging sessions"
	default 2000
	help
	  The radio cannot scan and range at the same time, so the two take turns.
	  This is the share given to discovering tags and refreshing their
	  addresses after a randomisation.

config CS_GATEWAY_STATUS_MS
	int "Milliseconds between gateway status heartbeats"
	default 30000
	help
	  A gateway with no tags in range is a normal state, not a fault, so it
	  reports that it is alive on a timer regardless of tag traffic. Mirrors
	  GATEWAY_STATUS_MS in the T-Echo gateway.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
